  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Additional heavy showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected through tonight, with scattered instances of flash flooding \npossible.\n\nGOES WV imagery shows a deep trough with a closed low across the \nOhio Valley this afternoon. A deep southerly flow with a plume of \nGulf moisture resides across the forecast area today. Surface \nanalysis shows a wavy cold front from Lake Erie to the Saint \nLawrence River which continues to be the focus for heavy showers and \nthunderstorms. Large scale ascent will aid diurnally driven \nconvection within a corridor of high moisture content and deep \n(>11kft) warm cloud depths. Surface based instability of 1000-1500 \nJ/kg and southerly winds parallel to the front will support \nbackbuilding/training storms as well as repeated rounds of storms. \nFurther destabilization and weak helicity across western NY will \nsupport a few severe thunderstorms with gusty winds and a \npossible tornado.\n\nA low pressure wave is expected to move along the frontal boundary \nacross the forecast area tonight, while remaining ahead of the \nmid level trough axis. The core of the highest PWATS will shift \neast tonight. Heavy showers and thunderstorms are expected \nacross the region with localized areas of heavy rainfall from \nthe Genesee Valley to the eastern Lake Ontario region. \n\nLatest guidance continues to suggest the axis of heaviest rain is \nacross far western New York to the Saint Lawrence River. Basin \naverage rainfall amounts will range from 1-2\" near this axis to 0.5-\n1\" farther east. Localized higher amounts of 3+ inches are possible.\nEfficient rainfall rates and repeated rounds of showers/storms \nfrom the slow moving system could result in localized flash \nflooding through tonight, especially for urban areas and poor \ndrainage areas. A Flood Watch is in effect through 2 AM \ntonight/Monday. \n\nConditions will improve from west to east on Monday. The surface low \nis expected to deepen as a shortwave trough drops south from Canada \nMonday. There remains uncertainty as to how fast showers move out \nof the forecast area. Kept a chance of showers and thunderstorms \nacross the eastern Lake Ontario region through Monday afternoon, \nalthough some models suggest it could dry out faster. High pressure \nbuilds across the region Monday night through Tuesday night \nresulting in dry weather. Northwest winds are expected Monday with \ngusts up to 28 mph along the southeast Lake Ontario shoreline. \n\nKey Message 2...Unsettled weather moves back into the region \nWednesday through the end of the week. \n\nThe chance for showers and thunderstorms return to the region \nWednesday through Friday. An upper level trough will move into the \nNorthern Plains/Great Lakes region and a return flow will bring \nmoisture northward. The synoptic pattern suggests deep southwesterly \nflow will persist during this time. While confidence is low \nregarding timing and intensity of showers and thunderstorms, chances \nare higher in the afternoon to evening.",
